## A B S T R A C T
Modified metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) doping with enzymes exhibit high enzyme stability and catalytic performance, which is a research hotspot in the field of enzyme-based sensing. Although the MOF-enzyme constitutes a 3D structure in the nanoscale, the macroscopic assembly configuration still stays in 1D or 2D structures, limiting sensing applications towards complex biological targets. Herein, the MOF-enzyme hybrid nanosystem was assembled into 3D porous conductive supports via a controllable physical embedding method, displaying high enzymatic loading, stability and cascade catalytic performance. The modified MOFs combing with enzymes served as a sensing reaction system, and the conductive hollow fiber membranes (HFMs) served as a functional platform. The multifunctional device integrates pumpless hydrodynamic transport, interconnected conductive polymer, and blood separation modules, showing fast capillary fluid flow, trace sampling $(3\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , high selectivity and accuracy. The linear sensing range was in $2{-}24~\mathrm{mM}$ glucose, $0.05{\scriptstyle-6}\ \mathrm{mM}$ lactic acid, and 0.1–10 mM cholesterol, respectively, with sensitivities of 24.2, 150, $73.6\ \mathrm{nA}\ \mathrm{mM}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, this strategy of modular assembly of biosensing array can easily implement multiplex metabolites detection simultaneously.
## 1. Introduction
The electrochemical sensors enable accurate and rapid detection of numerous analytes with the multifunctional combination of target molecules screening, specific recognition, signal transduction, and readout (Dincer et al., 2019; Ling et al., 2018). In order to achieve the sensitive response and accurate detection of analytes, the sensors need to assemble with nanomaterials to construct electrode interfaces. Among them, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) acting as enzyme immobilization matrices have attracted immense attention, due to the large accessible surface areas, and nanoporous structures (Li et al. 2020a, 2020b; Lu et al., 2020; Shieh et al., 2015). For example, Mohammad et al. combine enzyme and MOF composites on flat poly­ dopamine/polyethyleneimine patterns to increase enzymes stability and sensitivity in biosensing application (Mohammad et al., 2019). Wang et al. reported that NiPd hollow nanoparticles and glucose oxidase (GOx) were simultaneously immobilized on the zeolitic imidazolate framework 8 (ZIF-8), exhibiting good bioactivity of GOx and high electrocatalytic activity for sensors (Wang et al., 2017). Furthermore, mesoporous metalloporphyrin iron can be introduced in the MOFs and the generated Fe-MOFs possess peroxidase-like catalytic activity. The modified MOFs acting as nanozymes can combine with various oxidases, which impart high cascade catalytic performance and advantageous properties as compared with natural enzymes (Jiang et al., 2018).
Although the MOF-enzyme constitutes a 3D confined space in the nanoscale, the assembly configuration on electrodes remains in 1D or 2D structures, limiting sensing applications towards complex biological targets (An et al., 2020; Bell et al., 2020; Chen et al., 2020; Ellis et al., 2019). The 1D or 2D electrode structures restrict the active components immobilization efficiency and stability, thus being prone to leak due to the small surface area and weak adhesion. Also, the planar electrode structures could not provide a sufficient surface area for molecular/ion diffusion and interactions, thus decreasing the signal transmission capability (Lin et al., 2019; Rebelo et al., 2019). To solve this problem,
Ma et al. reported a three-dimensional (3D) amphiphilic aerogel as a support with high surface area and porosity, which showed high cata­ lytic performance in glucose detection (Ma et al., 2020a). Li et al. syn­ thesized a 3D conductive polyaniline hydrogels-based biosensor to obtain sensitive and rapid detection of human metabolites (Li et al. 2015, 2018). The 3D electrode structures facilitate the fluid diffusion and eliminate enzyme escape for electrochemical sensor applications (Chu et al., 2020; Luo et al., 2019; Park et al., 2019).
Nevertheless, it remains a challenge in how to effectively couple functional nanomaterial such as MOF-enzyme with a micro-scale device. The traditional assembly technologies include wax printing (Chiang et al., 2019), photolithography (Yu et al., 2011), inkjet etching (Alfadhel et al., 2018), cutting and shaping (de Oliveira et al., 2019; Shangguan et al., 2018), and chemical modification (Wu et al., 2013), which could lead to the leaking of active components from electrode’s surface, as well as limit the efficient collection of electrical signals. In addition, the conventional 3D electrodes lack of versatility, such as the ability to separate analytes from biological fluids, which lead to bias and incon­ sistent results (Park et al., 2019). Therefore, it is urgent to develop a multifunctional biosensing platform that takes into account the fixation of nano-material and the micro-scale electrode structure.
In this work, a novel MOF-enzyme hybrid nanosystem was assembled into 3D micro-porous electrodes via a controllable physical embedding pattern, as shown in Fig. 1. Firstly, the Fe(III) ions were doped into a representative MOF, UiO-66, giving the production peroxidase-like na­ ture (Jiang et al., 2018; Li et al., 2019; Xu et al., 2019; Zhao et al., 2019). The introduction of nanozymes (Fe-UiO-66) with various oxidases shows high cascade catalytic performance (Feng et al., 2012; Liu et al., 2019; Lu et al., 2015; Shi et al., 2011). Secondly, 3D hollow fiber membranes (HFMs) served as supports not only provide large surface area, but also use gradient porous structures for physical filtration. The conductive polyaniline nanoparticles (PANI) were in-situ synthesized on HFM supports and the MOF-enzyme was assembled in the pores via physical embedding method. The numerous microporous spaces in HFMs facilitate higher packing density of MOF-enzyme entrapped in confined spaces. In addition, the HFMs displayed a good separation performance to complex fluid such as blood. The nanostructures of conductive interconnected networks served as anchors to contact with cascade catalytic MOF-enzyme system, increasing the capability of signal transduction and collection of biosensors. The MOF-enzyme hybrid nanosystem decorated with 3D conductive hollow fiber membranes could extend to multiplex biosensing array via controllable embedding patterns.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and characterizations
Glucose oxidase (GOx, $>100\mathrm{u/mg}$ , $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ obtained from Aspergillus niger was supplied by Sangon Biotech Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). Lactate Oxidase (L $\scriptstyle\mathbf{)x},\geq90\mathbf{u}/\mathbf{mg}$ , $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) from brevibacterium was supplied from Heowns Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd (Tianjin, China). Esterase cholesterol (ChEt, $50~{\mathrm{u/mg}}$ , $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ from Pseudomonas Sp. and choles­ terol oxidase (ChOx, $19.1\mathrm{\u/mg}$ , $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ from brevibacterium were purchased from Yuanye Bio-Technology Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). Aniline (ANI), sulfuric acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}}S O_{4}$ , $96\%)$ , ammonium persulfate (APS), triton X-100, isopropanol, phosphate buffer solution (PBS, pH 7.4), $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB), $^{4^{\prime},6}$ -diamidino-2-phenylindole (DAPI), Rhodamine B (RhB), and fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich Company (St. Louis, MO, USA). Poly­ sulfone (PSF) powder, polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP–K17), polyethylene glycol (PEG-200), chloroplatinic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}P t C l_{6})}$ ), D- $(+)$ -glucose, uric acid (UA, $299\%$ , ascorbic acid (AA, $\geq99.7\%$ , lactic acid (LA, ${\geq}85\%$ ), and cholesterol were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Milli-Q-water was prepared by ultrafiltration of distilled water to 18.2 $\mathtt{M Q}\mathtt{c m}$ by an ELGA Classic UF system (Veolia Water Systems, France). Other chemicals at analytical grade were supplied by the Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China).
The surface morphology and structure were characterized by a scanning electron microscope (FESEM, Hitachi S-4800, Japan). The elemental analysis, contents and distribution were tested by Energydispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX, Oxford X-max 80), X-ray photo­ electron spectroscopy (XPS, PHI 5000c, PerkinElmer Instruments, USA), and confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM, LeicaTCS-SP5, Ger­ many). The size and zeta potential of particles were obtained using Zetasizer Nano ZS (Malvern Instruments) at ambient temperature. The cascade reaction activity was measured by UV–vis spectrometer (UV1800, Shimadzu). Electrochemical measurements were carried out by an electrochemical workstation (CHI 660) at room temperature.
## 2.2. Fabrication of the nanostructured conductive PANI-HFM
According to our previous study (Huang et al., 2020; Ma et al., 2020b; Wu et al. 2020, 2021; Zhou et al., 2019), a hollow fiber mem­ brane (HFM) was fabricated with gradient pore size $(<100\ \mathrm{nm}-3\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ , good hydrophilicity and high flux. The detailed procedure is provided in the Supporting Information. To endow a conductive property, conduc­ tive polymers were in situ synthesized on the scaffold of HFMs. First, the porous HFMs were incubating in an ANI solution for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The mixture solution of APS $(0.25~\mathrm{M})$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ (0.5 M) were stirred for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Next, the $\mathrm{\DeltaAPS/H_{2}S O_{4}}$ solution was added in the ANI solution, which PANI polymers were synthesized on HFM scaffolds in $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Finally, the modified HFM was washed three times with Milli-Q-water and dried at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Thus, the nanostructured conductive PANI-HFMs were obtained, retaining 3D gradient porous structures.
## 2.3. Fabrication of UiO-66 NPs and Fe-UiO-66 NPs
$33\mathrm{mgNH_{2}}$ -BDC and $31\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}$ were separately dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ and $10~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF, followed by mixing in a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ flask and adding $4~\mathrm{mL}$ HOAc at $123^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , the UiO-66 NPs were formed and collected by centrifugation at $7000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ and washed four times with DMF and four times with acetone over a total period of $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and then the obtained NPs were dried under vacuum.
To fabricate Fe-UiO-66 NPs, $20~\mathrm{mg}$ UiO-66 NPs and $40\mathrm{\mg\FeCl_{3}}$ were dispersed into $5~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF, respectively. Then the two solutions were mixed in a $25~\mathrm{mL}$ flask and heated in an oil bath at $105^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with magnetic stirring. After $^{12\mathrm{~h},}$ , Fe-UiO-66 NPs were obtained by centri­ fugation at $7000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ and washed four times with DMF and four times with acetone over a total period of $48\mathrm{{h}}$ . Finally, the Fe-UiO66 NPs were dried under vacuum for the next experiments.
## 2.4. Fabrication of the enzyme-Fe-UiO-66 $@$ PANI-HFM biosensors
Finally, we integrated conductive gradient porous membranes with enzyme-Fe-UiO-66 NPs to construct an enzyme based biosensing plat­ form. Approximately $1\mathrm{cm}$ length of the enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM was dipped in wax to block the conductive polymer. A platinum wire was inserted into the wax blocked lumen in HFMs as the reference electrode. The rest part of the PANI-HFM was used as the working electrode.
## 2.5. Kinetic study and cascade reaction
In order to investigate the reaction kinetic of the enzyme-Fe-UiO66@PANI-HFM, steady-state kinetics assays toward TMB oxidation were carried out in time course mode by monitoring the absorbance change at $652\mathrm{nm}$ by UV–vis spectrophotometer. Take GOx for example, GOx-MOF $@$ PANI-HFMs were fabricated according to the above mentioned. 2 cm GOx-MOF $@$ PANI-HFMs were added into $500~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ace­ tate buffer (pH 4.0, 0.1 mM) containing $1\ \mathrm{mM}$ TMB, different concen­ trations of glucose from 0.3 to $7~\mathrm{mM}$ . The kinetic parameters of the catalytic reaction were determined on the basis of the Michaelis-Menten equation (Feng et al., 2012; Lu et al., 2015),
$$
\frac{1}{\mathrm{v}}=\frac{\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}}{\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{max}}}\frac{1}{[\mathrm{S}]}+\frac{1}{\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{max}}}
$$
where v is the initial velocity of the reaction, $\mathtt{V}_{\mathrm{max}}$ is the maximal rate of reaction, [S] is the substrate concentration, and $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ is the MichaelisMenten constant. The $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{m a x}}$ were obtained from LineweaverBurk plots (Li et al., 2019).
In order to investigate the cascade catalytic performance of the biosensors, 2 cm GOx-MOF $@$ PANI-HFMs were added into $500~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ PBS buffer $\left(\mathrm{pH}~7.4\right)$ ) containing $1\ \mathrm{mM}$ TMB, different concentrations of glucose from 0.2 to $\mathbf{8\mM}$ . The mixture system was reacted for $10\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature, and then the mixture solution was measured by UV–vis spectrometer at $652\mathrm{nm}$ . All tests were measured at least 5 times.
## 2.6. Electrochemical detection in PBS solution
In this experiment, glucose and LA were dissolved in a PBS solution $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ , respectively. Cholesterol was dissolved in a mixture of iso­ propanol, Triton X-100, and PBS $\mathrm{(pH}7.4)$ in a volume ratio of 5:2:43. According to different target analytes, corresponding enzymes were used in the preparation of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFMs. Ampero­ metric current-time curves were recorded by an electrochemical work­ station. In this detection, the designed electrodes were put in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ PBS under $0.5{\mathrm{~V}}$ working potential with the addition of different concen­ trations of glucose, LA or cholesterol every 600 s. All tests were measured at least 3 times.
## 2.7. Multiplexed electrochemical detection in real blood samples
A coin-sized polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) substrate containing microfluidic channels, multiple enzyme-Fe-UiO-66 $@$ PANI-HFMs, and a portable electrochemical workstation assembled together as electrode arrays (Figure S1). In the novel arrays, the reference and working electrodes were integrated on a single membrane as a two-electrode electrochemical system. Enzyme-Fe-UiO-66 $@$ PANI-HFMs with different enzymes were used for one-time multiplexed electrochemical detection. In the detection of human biomarkers, approximately $3\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of blood rose inside the lumen of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66 $@$ PANI-HFMs by microcapillary force within 3s. Blood cells were rejected in the inner surface, while smaller molecules diffused freely to the outer surface of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFMs for electrochemical reactions. Whole blood samples were provided by affiliated hospital of Hangzhou Normal University in compliance with the relevant laws and institutional guidelines. All blood samples were analyzed by YSI Biochemistry analyzer to obtain the reference concentration. All tests were measured at least 5 times.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterizations of the enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANIHFM
The UiO-66 NPs were first fabricated via a typical method as re­ ported (Xu et al., 2019; Zhao et al., 2019). Subsequently, the Fe(III) ions were doped into UiO-66 by coordinatively binding to the Zr-oxo clusters, and the Fe-UiO-66 NPs were synthesized. The photographs (Figure S2) and SEM images (Figure S3) of UiO-66 and Fe-UiO-66 demonstrated that the introduction of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ lead to slight increase in size (average size of Fe-UiO-66: $300\ \mathrm{nm},$ ), while maintaining original octahedron-shape morphology. Comparing with the powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) pro­ file (Figure S4), the high crystallinity of UiO-66 is maintained after $\bar{\mathrm{Fe}}^{3+}$ doping. The nitrogen sorption isotherms (Figures S5a) indicated that there are good adsorption capacity and microporous structures of Fe-UiO-66. According to the pore size distribution (Figures S5b) and the large Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) specific surface area analysis, there are narrow MOF NPs distribution with an average size of $4.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ and large specific surface area with $871\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of Fe-UiO-66, facilitating the controllable MOFs spatial modulation and free diffusion of small mo­ lecular substrates, such as hydrogen peroxide. Also, the Fe-UiO-66 could act as enzyme protective carriers, which is shown in Figures S6 and Table S1. Comparing with Fe-UiO-66, the nitrogen content of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66 increased from 6.02 to $15.68\%$ , due to the hydrogen bonding interaction between enzymes and Fe-UiO-66.
The promising concept of MOF coupled with enzymes is applied as a cascade catalytic module in biosensor filed. Take the glucose sensing as an example, in the presence of GOx and oxygen, glucose in the body fluid can be selectively oxidized into gluconic acid and hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ . The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can be oxidize to ⋅OH in the presence of Fe-UiO-66 with peroxidase-like catalytic activity (Luo et al., 2019). To realize effective immobilization and entrapment of enzymes, microporous PANI-HFMs containing gradient structures were fabricated to create 3D porous scaffold. As shown in Fig. 2a and Fig. 2b, the increased nitrogen content and new peaks at 3300, 2359, 1294, $1120~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of PANI-HFM confirm that the PANI NPs were synthesized in the HFMs scaffold. The GOx-Fe-UiO-66 NPs were entrapped in the pores of PANI-HFMs via controllable physical embedding method. Comparing with the FTIR spectrum, there are similar infrared spectral peaks between PANI-HFM and GOx-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM (Fig. 2b), indicating the GOx-Fe-UiO-66 NPs were encapsulated in the pores without adsorption on outer surface of PANI-HFM. The surface morphologies of blank HFM, PANI-HFM and GOx-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM were shown in Fig. 2c. The SEM images of blank HFM (Fig. $2\mathbf{c}_{1})$ and PANI-HFM (Fig. $2\mathbf{c}_{2})$ revealed that the PANI nanoparticles synthesized on the membrane scaffolds uniformly and formed an interconnected network, retaining the hollow fiber structure and ordered radial micropores. Based on the retained gradient microporous structure, the GOx-Fe-UiO-66 with nanometer-scale can be entrapped in the pores of conductive PANI-HFMs (Fig. $2\mathbf{c}_{3})$ , facilitating to efficient immobilization and sta­ bility of enzyme-nanozyme system. To further investigate the spatial distribution of GOx-Fe-UiO-66 in the PANI-HFM scaffolds, the fluores­ cence images analysis were performed in Fig. 2d. Compared with blank PANI-HFM, the FITC labeled GOx-Fe-UiO-66 were observed in whole scaffold, indicating the high loading capacity and uniform distribution in the membrane pores. Overall, the novel 3D electrochemical bio­ sensors integrate MOF-enzyme cascade catalytic module, microporous immobilization membrane module, as well as nanostructured conduc­ tive networks module for sensing (Fig. 1).
## 3.2. Performances of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM
The enzymatic loading amount of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM was determined by bradford assay with absorbance changes at 595 nm. The enzymatic loading amount (Fig. 3a) of enzyme-Fe-UiO$66@$ PANI-HFM is $2.54~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-3}$ , which is 9 times higher than of free GOx and Fe-UiO-66 coating on the PANI-HFM $(0.28\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ , possibly due to large specific surface area of the 3D porous scaffold and effective space utilization by the novel enzyme immobilization strategy. As shown in Fig. 3b and c, the wash stability and storage stability of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM were evaluated. The cascade catalytic activity of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM kept at $90.6\%$ after $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ washing, while the activity of free $\mathbf{GOx}$ and Fe-UiO-66 coated on the PANI-HFM decreased to $5.7\%$ . The cascade catalytic activity of enzymeFe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM maintained $64.6\%$ when storing for 7 days at room temperature, while the activity of free GOx and Fe-UiO-66 coated on the PANI-HFM decreased to $19.3\%$ . The selectivity against potential interference was also evaluated (Fig. 3d) under the same experimental conditions. Compared with glucose, there were negligible absorbance changes at $652\mathrm{nm}$ (oxidized TMB) by uric acid (UA), ascorbic acid (AA), cholesterol, and lactic acid (LA). The excellent selectivity can be attributed to the high specificity of enzymes toward respective substrates.
Fast accurate and effective sampling in microfluidic analytical plat­ forms are essential for point-of-care testing (POCT) sensors. The average flux values (Fig. 3e) of enzyme-Fe-UiO- $66@$ PANI-HFM remained at $3712\mathrm{L}/(\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{h})$ at $10\mathrm{min}$ under $0.1\mathrm{MPa}$ . The high-throughput indicates a good absorbing capacity for fluid, such as interstitial fluid, sweat, saliva, tears, and blood. Among them, biochemical testing of blood plays a critical role in clinical diagnosis, which contains various human me­ tabolites, glucose, uric acid, cholesterol, and triglycerides, etc. However, the blood cells contained in whole blood will interfere with electro­ chemical detection and lead to biased results. The $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ pores in the inner surface of PANI-HFM can filter out blood cells which range from 6 to $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , improving the accuracy in biosensors (Chu et al., 2016; Park et al., 2019; Wu et al., 2021). Herein, enzyme-Fe-UiO-66 $@$ PANI-HFM showed excellent blood separation performance, as evidenced in Fig. 3f and g. Compared with whole blood, the cells number decreased from 4639 to nearly $0\ {\upmu\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ and the absorption spectrum peaks repre­ senting blood cells disappeared after blood separation by enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM. Furthermore, the decreased levels of hemoglobin, porphyrin and potassium ions levels (Table S2) also indi­ cated good blood separation by enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM. In addition, hydrodynamic property of fluid transportation in enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM was examined in Fig. 3h and i. There was a $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ droplet being absorbed into $3~\mathrm{\mm}$ lumen of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM, showing about $5.4~\mathrm{cm}$ absorption height by capillary force. According to a fluorescent tracing method, 10 μL of RhB (yellow) was absorbed into DAPI (blue) modified enzyme-Fe-UiO-66 $@$ PANI-HFM for 3.7 s without extra pump. The re­ sults displayed the hybrid hydrodynamic transportation, where a macroscale capillary exists in the lumen and a microscale fluid diffusion occurs in gradient pores, facilitating fast capillary fluid flow and uniform fluid diffusion. Compared to 2D flat membranes, there is higher capillary height in 3D hollow fiber membranes, which helps blood adsorption and diffusion. All hydrodynamic testing results demonstrated fast capillary fluid flow $_{(<3\mathrm{~s)}}$ , trace volume sampling $(3~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ and uniform fluid diffusion, providing the possibility for accurate analytes detection in POCT applications.
## 3.3. Kinetic study and cascade catalytic reaction
To investigate the cascade catalytic performance of the GOx-Fe-UiO$66@$ PANI-HFM, TMB was applied as a typical peroxidase substrate. In this reaction, GOx catalyzed the oxidation of glucose to form gluconic acid and hydrogen peroxide. Then, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reacted with TMB to form a blue complex in the presence of peroxidase-like Fe-UiO-66, showing a maximum adsorption peak at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The time-course absorbance of GOx-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM was performed in Fig. 4a at $\mathrm{pH}4$ under room temperature (Figure S6). According to Lineweaver-Burk plot, the Michaelis-Menten constant $\mathrm{(K_{m})}$ and maximum reaction velocity $\mathrm{(V_{max})}$ were obtained to be 1.21 and $3.08\times10^{-6}\mathrm{M}\mathrm{S}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4b). The low $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ and high $\mathtt{V}_{\mathrm{max}}$ values indicate good affinity of substrate to catalyst, and good cascade catalytic activity. To further explore cascade catalytic performance, the glucose detection of GOx-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM was carried out in the presence of TMB, as shown in Fig. 4c and d. There were good linear relationship in $\mathsf{1-8~m M}$ glucose concentration range with a correlation efficiency of 0.991, and good sensitivity in $200{-}1000~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ glucose concentration range with a correlation efficiency of 0.967, respectively. The linear range implies high sensitivity and good accuracy of the new biosensor, which is a promising candidate for bioassays in POCT.
## 3.4. Electrochemical performance of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM biosensors
The continuous 3D networks with nanostructured conductive mate­ rials improve electrochemical property towards oxidase enzyme based system. As shown in Fig. 5, glucose, LA, and cholesterol biosensors were fabricated by entrapping GOx, LOx, and ChEt/ChOx in conductive HFM scaffold. The electrochemical performance of enzyme-Fe-UiO$66@$ PANI-HFM electrodes were first verified in PBS solution. Linear relationships were extracted with correlation efficiencies of 0.999, 0.999, 0.991 in $2{-}24~\mathrm{mM}$ glucose, $0.05{\scriptstyle-6}\ \mathrm{mM}$ LA, $0.1\mathrm{-}10\ \mathrm{mM}$ choles­ terol, corresponding sensitivities reached 24.2, 150, $73.6\mathrm{nAmM^{-1}}$ . The (limit of detection) LOD for glucose, lactic acid, and cholesterol were as low as 0.13, 0.003, and $0.006~\upmu\mathrm{M}.$ respectively. The novel biosensors cover required detection concentrations in real blood samples with desirable catalytic activities and high sensitivity, because of the suffi­ cient electrolyte ion contact and effective entrapment of MOF-enzyme in the microporous scaffolds for reliable electrochemical sensing.
## 3.5. Multiplexed metabolites monitoring of real blood sample
According to different target analytes, different enzymes were introduced in the preparation of enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFMs. As shown in Fig. 6a and b, a PDMS substrate containing suitable microchannels was fabricated. Comparing with the size of fifty cents (diam­ eter: $20.5~\mathrm{{mm}},$ , the similar size of PDMS substrate implied a portable biosensing application. Then, multiple enzyme-Fe-UiO-66 $@$ PANI-HFMs were assembled on a PDMS substrate as arrays for disposable electro­ chemical tests, including blood separation, cascade catalytic reaction, and readout areas (Fig. 6c). In the practical application, approximately $3~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of blood was absorbed into each lumen containing different enzyme-Fe-UiO-66 $@$ PANI-HFMs by microcapillary force and liquid diffusion (Fig. 6d). Blood cells were rejected in the inner surface, while smaller molecules diffused freely through the pores of enzyme-Fe-UiO$66@$ PANI-HFMs for cascade reactions. As shown in Fig. 6e, glucose, lactic acid, and cholesterol levels were examined simultaneously in enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM assays in blood samples from four pa­ tients. The normal concentrations of glucose, lactic acid, and cholesterol should be 3.61–6.11, 0.5–1.7 and less than $5.17\mathrm{mM}$ , respectively. The patients 1 and 4 suffer hyperglycemia (6.92 and $9.46~\mathrm{mM},$ , and patients 3 suffers hypercholesterolemia $(5.43\ \mathrm{mM})$ detected by enzyme-Fe-UiO$66@$ PANI-HFM assays. In addition, the lactic acid levels of four pa­ tients are in the range of normal levels. Comparing with the glucose, lactic acid, and cholesterol levels measured by YSI biochemical ana­ lyzers, errors within $8.4\%$ , $8.2\%$ , and $6.3\%$ by our sensors, respectively, suggesting the novel assay can realize accurate and sensitive multi­ plexed sensing monitoring in real samples. In this array, a coin-sized PDMS substrate was used to construct microfluidic channels, a trace amount of blood was detected, multiple enzyme-Fe-UiO- $66@$ PANIHFMs were used as separation and reaction area, and a portable elec­ trochemical workstation worked as readout area. Therefore, the new 3D biosensors can develop into a portable system.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, we reported novel enzyme-Fe-UiO-66@PANI-HFM biosensors with MOF-enzyme hybrid nanosystem assembling into conductive microporous HFMs. The modified MOFs combing with en­ zymes served as a sensing reaction system, and the conductive HFMs served as a functional platform. The MOF-enzyme were assembled into 3D porous conductive supports via a controllable physical embedding pattern, displaying high cascade catalytic performance in the presence of specific analytes. The porous HFMs integrate liquid transport module, signal transduction, and separation modules for sensing application. The nanostructures of conductive interconnected networks served as anchors to contact with cascade catalytic MOF-enzyme, increasing the capability of signal transduction and collection of biosensors. Unique structures of HFMs facilitate liquid transportation in a lumen, uniform fluid diffusion in gradient pores, as well as excellent blood separation performance. Such assembled 3D biosensors showed good stability, good selectivity and high sensitivity in towards various analytes both PBS and blood samples. Several multifunctional devices can be assembled as an array to realize multiplex metabolites detection simultaneously, which is helpful for human comprehensive physical examination.
